Fibroids
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Menorrhagia/prolonged menses, Pelvic pressure, Firm irregularly enlarged uterus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-016
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, intractable pelvic pain indicative of fibroid degeneration, accompanied by urinary retention.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of excruciating pelvic pain and inability to void over the past 6 hours. Urgent assessment was required to rule out an acute surgical abdomen (such as fibroid torsion or red degeneration), temporarily decompress the bladder, and administer potent parenteral analgesia to prevent emergency transport before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The pressure in my pelvis is agonizing, and the pain is so sharp now that I cannot push to pass any urine.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began progressively 48 hours ago with a dull dragging ache, escalating sharply 6 hours ago to acute, severe, focal pelvic pain. The patient reports a two-year history of heavy menstrual bleeding (menorrhagia) leading to rapid soaking of sanitary products, and prolonged menstrual periods lasting more than a week. Over the past month, she has experienced a constant sensation of pelvic pressure and fullness. Today, she noted increased urinary frequency that rapidly progressed to acute difficulty emptying the bladder due to anterior compression. She also reports severe dyspareunia previously, and a history of subfertility.
Functional Status: Bedbound, actively writhing in severe pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of acute bladder distension requiring catheterization.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is pale, diaphoretic, and clutching her lower abdomen in significant distress.
Physical Exam: Abdominal and pelvic examination reveals a firm, irregularly enlarged uterus palpable up to the level of the umbilicus, equivalent to a 16-week gestation. There is exquisite point tenderness at the right fundal pole, highly suspicious for acute fibroid degeneration. Suprapubic dullness to percussion indicates a distended bladder. No rebound peritonism or guarding in the lower quadrants.
Environmental Observations: Clear path to the bathroom, well-lit environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Took maximum daily doses of paracetamol and ibuprofen with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uterine Fibroids (Suspected acute red degeneration/torsion with compressive urinary retention) - Status: Exacerbated/Acute
Differentials:

Ovarian Torsion - Unlikely given the physical exam revealing a firmly enlarged, irregular central uterine mass rather than a discrete, unilateral adnexal mass.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out due to the absence of right lower quadrant peritonism (McBurney's point tenderness) and lack of fever or gastrointestinal symptoms.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out definitively by a negative point-of-care urine beta-hCG test.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, acute focal pelvic pain and impending urinary retention superimposed on a history of menorrhagia and a known large pelvic mass. Objective physical examination confirmed a highly tender, firm, irregularly enlarged uterus and a distended bladder. The acute, intractable nature of the pain and the mechanical obstruction of the urinary tract required immediate after-hours evaluation for potent intramuscular analgesia and potential catheterization. Failing to intervene before the next in-hours period would have resulted in acute renal injury secondary to obstruction and inevitable emergency department presentation for unmanageable pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg and subcutaneous fentanyl 50mcg for acute pain control. Performed a single, gentle in-and-out catheterization yielding 500mL of clear urine, instantly relieving the compressive retention. Prescribed oral tramadol 50mg every 6 hours as needed.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain regular bladder emptying while the fibroids are inflamed, attempt to void every two hours on a strict schedule, leaning completely forward on the toilet; achieving successful, low-pressure emptying will reinforce the habit of timed, positional voiding. To manage heavy bleeding during your cycle, track saturated pads daily; observing the objective data will assist the gynecologist in prioritizing your surgical intervention.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow morning to arrange an expedited formal pelvic ultrasound and gynecological surgical referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if you become completely unable to pass urine again, if the pain returns to an unbearable level despite the tramadol, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute fibroid pain can mimic surgical emergencies; if symptoms worsen rapidly, immediate hospital reassessment is necessary.
Clinical Course: The injections will provide profound relief for several hours, and emptying the bladder has removed the pressure. The oral medications must be taken strictly to maintain this relief.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you cannot pass urine for more than 6 hours, or if you experience sudden, catastrophic pelvic pain.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A large fibroid on the top of your uterus has likely outgrown its blood supply, causing severe inflammation and pain (red degeneration). Its size was also physically pressing your bladder shut. The injection blocks the severe nerve pain, and emptying your bladder manually has removed the immediate mechanical blockage.
Actionable Steps: Take the tramadol at the earliest sign of the pain returning. Attempt to empty your bladder completely every two hours using the leaning technique, and see your regular GP first thing tomorrow.

